e could:\n\n```python\nmsg = super().format(record)\n```\nthen apply truncation to `record.message` portion within msg? Simpler: compute the truncated message ourselves and temporarily use a copy? But restriction says must not rewrite shared record's msg/args/exc_info/stack_info. It doesn't forbid setting record.message, but same record passed to full handler afterwards — record.message may be recomputed? Actually for a subsequent handler with a different formatter, super().format() will recompute record.message = record.getMessage(), so truncation of record.message doesn't leak as long as we don't touch msg/args. But safer: don't mutate; build the string ourselves.\n\nApproach in format():\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    ...\n```\nThen the multi-line handling uses record.message. Hmm truncation must occur before multiline prefixing? Requirement: \"沿用原有多行前缀、时间精度、颜色及异常处理\" - the truncation should apply to message body only, not prefix. Easiest: truncate `record.message` before calling super().format? But then exc_info would still be appended by super(). Hmm, but we can't mutate record.message? The spec forbids msg, args, exc_info, stack_info — record.message is fine to set but it's a bit hacky, and subsequent handlers recompute it. Actually logging.Formatter.format sets record.message = record.getMessage() each time. So mutating record.message is harmless.\n\nBut subtlety: multi-line handling does `msg.find(record.message)`; if we truncated record.message, find works only if the truncated text appears in msg — it does since super().format used the truncated message. So mutating record.message to truncated version before super().format works and keeps multi-line prefix intact.\n\nHowever truncation must happen after %s substitution — record.getMessage() does that. So:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, 'keep_full_message', None) is not True:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        truncated = self._truncate(message)\n        if truncated != message:\n            # don't touch record.msg/args; only render a derived message\n            ...\n```\n\nHmm, mutating record.message before super().format: super().format re-sets record.message = record.getMessage(), overwriting. So can't. Alternative: compute msg via super().format into string, then replace the message portion. Better: compute the full formatted msg with super().format(record) first, then build truncated version by re-formatting? Simpler robust approach: temporarily set record.message then... no.\n\nAlternative: create a shallow copy of the record for formatting? Cleanest: use a copy.copy of record with msg replaced — but copy of LogRecord may lose attributes? copy.copy copies __dict__, fine. But requirement \"不能改写共享原记录\" — copy is allowed. Yet exc_info references same, fine.\n\nApproach:\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    truncated = self._truncate(message) if budget else message\n    if truncated is not message:  # need truncation\n        record = copy.copy(record)  # careful: can not import copy? copy is stdlib but import restrictions list allowed imports for action code, not source. Source can import copy.\n```\nWait imports allowed list is for my action code, business source can import anything stdlib.\n\nBut copying LogRecord: logging.Formatter.format uses self.formatMessage(record) with record.message; also record.exc_text caching, record.stack_info. copy.copy gives new dict copy (shallow) so exc_text assignments won't propagate? Actually shallow copy creates new object with same __dict__ dict copy; setting record.message on copy won't affect original. Good.\n\nSimpler still: compute the message prefix manually:\n```python\nmsg = super().format(record)\nif truncated != message and record.message:\n    prefix = msg[:msg.find(record.message)]\n    suffix = msg[msg.find(record.message)+len(record.message):]\n    msg = prefix + truncated + suffix\n```\nSince super().format inserts record.message verbatim, index-based replacement works. Multi-line check then uses record.message (original, which appears in msg? No — after replacement, original message may not appear). The multi-line alignment uses record.message to find prefix; but we can keep prefix computed before replacement. Let's restructure:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    full_message = record.message\n    if full_message:\n        start = msg.find(full_message)\n        prefix = msg[:start] if start >= 0 else None\n        ...\n```\nHmm messy when message appears in prefix.\n\nI think the copy approach is cleanest and robust. But careful with exc_info: copy keeps exc_info; super().format will compute traceback and cache in record.exc_text — with copy, caching is on copy, which is fine.\n\nAlso stack_info included by super().format after message, so prefix/suffix keep it. Good — budget doesn't limit traceback or stack_info.\n\nBut we must compute truncation only for message body, and `...<omitted>...` counts. Implementation:\n\n```python\n_TRUNCATE_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _truncate_message(self, message: str) -> str:\n    budget = self._max_message_chars\n    if budget is None or len(message) <= budget:\n        return message\n    remaining = budget - len(_TRUNCATE_MARKER)\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return message[:budget]\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return f\"{message[:head]}{_TRUNCATE_MARKER}{message[len(message) - tail:] if tail else ''}\"\n```\nBudget >=32 and marker 15 chars so remaining>=17, fine (example: budget 32 -> remaining 17, head 9, tail 8 ✓).\n\nNow where to truncate: after super().format? Let's do the copy approach:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n    max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n    if max_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        truncated = _truncate(message, max_chars)\n        if truncated != message:\n            record = copy.copy(record)\n            record.message = truncated\n            # need to render with the new message: use formatMessage\n            ...\n```\nHmm with copy, super().format would recompute record.message = record.getMessage() which returns original msg since msg/args unchanged. So need to bypass. Instead do:\n\n```python\n            record = copy.copy(record)\n            record.msg = truncated\n            record.args = None\n```\nThat mutates the copy — allowed since it's not the shared original. That's clean. Actually copying and setting msg/args on the copy is fully correct.\n\nBut wait: \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info\" - the copy is not shared. Fine.\n\nHmm, but getMessage could raise if args mismatch; not our concern.\n\nSo:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n        if max_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            truncated = self._truncate(message, max_chars)\n            if truncated != message:\n                record = copy.copy(record)\n                record.msg = truncated\n                record.args = None\n                record.message = truncated\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n```\nSetting record.message before super().format is unnecessary since formatMessage sets it. But harmless — actually super().format sets record.message = record.getMessage() = truncated (since msg/args replaced). Fine, skip explicit set.\n\nThen multiline and color logic preserved.\n\nAlso need `import copy`. And env import for _setup_logger: `from deepinfer import environ`? logger.py currently imports nothing from deepinfer. Check for circular imports: environ.py imports os, threading, datetime only — safe. Use `from deepinfer